## Code and docs conventions

- Style follows the [Google Python Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html). Line length is @@line_length@@ and strings use double quotes (`pyproject.toml`, `[tool.ruff]`). Ruff enforces import order and Google-convention docstrings.
- Docstrings use the Google format with `Args:`, `Returns:`, `Yields:` and `Raises:` sections. Skip repeating types that the signature already annotates.
- Tests live in `tests/` and mirror the source tree: `@@src_hint@@/billing/invoice.py` is tested in `tests/billing/test_invoice.py`. Use `pytest.mark.parametrize`, `pytest.raises(..., match=...)`, `tmp_path` and `monkeypatch`. Shared fixtures go in `conftest.py`.
- Credentials never appear in code, configs or logs. Read them from environment variables. Document each variable in `.env.example` with a placeholder value.

## Testing and validation

Before finishing a change:
- `make lint`, `make lint-yaml` and `make typecheck` must pass.
- `make unit-tests` must pass, and new behavior needs a test that fails without the change.
- Do not lower the coverage gate or add `# noqa` to get a change through. Fix the cause.

## Pull request guidance

- Follow `.github/commit_template.md` (Conventional Commits) and fill out `.github/pull_request_template.md`.
- Review your own diff against the checklist in the PR template before requesting review.

## Boundaries

- Never read, edit or commit `.env` or any `.env.*` file except `.env.example`. They hold live secrets.
- Never hardcode credentials, tokens or connection strings.
- Do not run deploy, publish or other shared or destructive commands without explicit confirmation.
- Ask for clarification when a missing decision blocks safe progress instead of guessing.
